---
name: pool_status
description: |
  Report the fleet's AI subscription pool balances — percent or units remaining in each quota window, reset times, and pool health (ok, capped, login-lapsed, error, unknown) — from the hourly pool cache, and produce the burn order (soonest expiry, most remaining first) that lane routing spends from. Use whenever a decision needs pool state: picking which subscription a coding lane should draw on, checking what quota is stranded or about to expire unused, verifying a pool is capped or its login has lapsed, or answering how much is left on a pool. Read-only: it reports state, it never launches work.
---

# Pool Status

Read the fleet's subscription pool balances from the hourly pool cache and present them as a burn order. The cache is refreshed every hour by the coordinator (Bossman VM) from the read-only subscription census; every fleet node carries a copy, so this works on any machine without running a census.

## Read the cache

Run the reader first:

```
python3 ~/.agents/skills/pool_status/scripts/pool_status.py
```

It finds the cache at the first of: `$POOL_CACHE`, `~/capabilities/pool-cache.json` (every fleet node), or the coordinator canonical copy `~/workspace/projects/fleet-ops/hidden_files/pool-cache.json` (Bossman VM). Add `--json` to get the raw cache for programmatic use.

The cache holds, per pool (chatgpt-gmail, chatgpt-live, chatgpt-proton, claude-max-gmail, claude-max-live, kimi-code, google-ai-ultra, zai-glm-coding, qwen-token-plan, muse-code-power, supergrok): a status, and per window the percent remaining (or units remaining/limit for metered pools like Kimi), the reset timestamp, and the observation time.

## Rules

- **Staleness:** if the reader flags the cache STALE (observed more than 2 hours ago), do not route a lane on it. On the coordinator VM, fall back to the live census via the lane_router helper (`~/.agents/skills/lane_router/scripts/pools.py`); on a fleet node, say the cache is stale and ask the coordinator for fresh numbers — the plan travels, not the data.
- **Unknown is not empty:** a pool with status `error`, `unknown`, or `login_lapsed` has an unreadable or unusable balance, never a zero balance. Never present it as spent or available; name the state and move on.
- **Capped is unavailable until reset:** a `capped` pool (or a window at 0 remaining) cannot be drawn on; note its reset time when a lane is worth deferring to it.
- **Login-only pools:** claude-max-*, qwen-token-plan, muse-code-power and supergrok expose login state, not usage percentages. Their absence from the burn order means "no meter", not "no quota".
- **Burn order:** spend from the top — the window resetting soonest with the most remaining is the quota most likely to expire unused. Quota that lapses is the waste this ordering exists to prevent.

## Present

Show the burn-order table the reader prints, then the per-pool status lines, calling out any capped, lapsed, error or unknown pool by name. Stop at reporting: launching a lane on a pool still needs the user's explicit confirmation for that lane (see the lane_router skill).

